; Singularity VR mod - settings
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HookUserPointerDraws
;
; UE3 submits dynamic geometry (particles, decals, sprites) through
; DrawPrimitiveUP / DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP. Those draws are not split per eye,
; so a centred one lands on the seam between the eye halves and disappears -
; the suspected cause of the chest and similar objects vanishing.
;
; Hooking them stopped the game reaching its menus, and a bisection found that
; level 1 (patch installed, forward immediately) works while level 2 (identical
; plus a single counter increment) does not.
;
; ---- run 27: the cause was found, and it was not threading ----
;
; The per-second perf log line had nine printf conversions and TEN arguments -
; the user-pointer counter was missing its %d, so %s formatted an int as a
; char*. That is harmless while the counter is zero and an access violation the
; moment it is not, which is exactly at level 2. The fault happened inside
; Log(), inside a held critical section, so the lock was never released and
; every later log call blocked - a hang, not a crash.
;
; The evidence had been in every log since run 23: "mono-fallback 0(null)".
;
; So level 3 should now simply work. The thread guard is kept regardless -
; issuing device state changes from a thread that does not own the device is
; wrong either way - and the log now reports whether these calls really do
; arrive off the render thread, which was only ever inferred.
;
;   0 = not hooked
;   1 = hooked, forwards immediately
;   2 = level 1 plus a counter        <- this is the one that used to hang
;   3 = full per-eye treatment        <- try this
;
; If it still hangs, set back to 0 and relaunch. No rebuild needed.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[Render]

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AutoResolution   -- inherit the resolution from the headset (run 59)
;
; Every other VR title takes its resolution from the headset. This one needed a
; hand-typed -ResX/-ResY from run 17 until now. With this on, you just launch
; the game.
;
;   Singularity.exe          <- that is the whole command
;
; ---- YOUR COMMAND LINE ALWAYS WINS ----
;
; If you pass -ResX yourself, nothing is injected and your value is used. The
; log says so explicitly:
;
;   auto-resolution: not injecting (you passed -ResX yourself, which always wins)
;
; ---- how it works, and why it is a CACHE ----
;
; This DLL is a d3d9.dll proxy, so the loader runs its DllMain before the exe's
; entry point - before the CRT reads GetCommandLineW and before UE3 parses it.
; A detour installed there is in place in time; anything later is not, because
; the engine has already read it.
;
; The size itself comes from the PREVIOUS run. The mod queries the headset every
; launch and writes the answer to AutoResX/AutoResY below; the next launch
; injects it. The alternative was spinning up a temporary OpenXR instance inside
; DllMain to ask live, which is possible but puts XR initialisation in the
; process startup path - where a failure means THE GAME DOES NOT LAUNCH.
;
; So: change your headset's render scale and the first run afterwards uses the
; old size, the next one is correct. One stale run beats a game that won't boot.
;
; The first ever run injects nothing, because there is no cache yet. Launch once
; with your own -ResX, or just launch and let it write the cache, then relaunch.
;
; Every failure path leaves the command line untouched: no cache, a nonsense
; value, a failed hook, or -ResX already present.
;
; Why the command line and not the backbuffer: run 14 proved that overriding the
; D3D9 backbuffer does NOT raise UE3's render resolution. The engine sizes its
; scene targets from its own config, so forcing the backbuffer desynchronises
; the two and breaks stereo. The resolution has to reach UE3's own settings.
;
;   1 = inject the cached headset size when you did not specify one <- default
;   0 = never touch the command line
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AutoResolution=1

; Written by the mod, not by you. The headset's per-eye width x2 (side by side)
; and its height, cached from the last run. Delete these to force a re-cache.
; Overwritten only when the headset's answer actually changes.
;AutoResX=4992
;AutoResY=2688

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The cold start is flat, as always - no head tracking, no 6-DOF, mono. Press
; F9, F10 and F1 individually as before, OR press BACKSPACE once for all three
; at once (and again to drop back to flat). BACKSPACE is just a shortcut for
; the same three keys; it changes nothing about how the game looks on launch.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; D3D9ExMode   -- the zero-copy prerequisite (run 38). OFF until proven.
;
; OpenXR has no D3D9 binding, so every frame must reach D3D11. Doing that
; without the CPU round-trip needs a SHARED SURFACE, which needs a D3D9Ex
; device, and D3D9Ex rejects D3DPOOL_MANAGED - which UE3 uses for essentially
; everything (spike 2: 10,454 MANAGED allocations against 47 DEFAULT).
;
; So this upgrades the device to Ex and translates the pool:
;
;   vertex + index buffers (7,149)   pool swap only. D3D9 allows Lock() on a
;                                    DEFAULT buffer - it stalls, but these are
;                                    locked once at load, not per frame.
;   textures/cube/volume (3,305)     pool swap PLUS a SYSTEMMEM shadow, because
;                                    LockRect on a DEFAULT texture genuinely
;                                    fails. The game is handed the DEFAULT
;                                    texture and its Lock/Unlock are redirected
;                                    to the shadow, which is uploaded with
;                                    UpdateTexture when unlocked after a write.
;
; What makes this safe rather than merely possible: D3D9Ex does NOT lose
; D3DPOOL_DEFAULT resources on Reset. On a plain device this translation would
; mean rebuilding every resource on every device-lost event.
;
; THIS IS STAGE 1. It changes only how resources are allocated - the frame is
; still delivered by the old CPU copy, so if it works the game should look and
; perform EXACTLY as before. That is the point: it isolates "does the wrapper
; work" from "does zero-copy work". Stage 2 switches the frame path.
;
; What to check in the log:
;
;   D3D9Ex pool translation: 3276 textures, 26 cube, 3 volume, 6258 vertex buf,
;     891 index buf | shadows 3305 (~NNN MB), shadow failures 0,
;     create failures 0, GetSurfaceLevel-on-shadowed 0
;
; Counts should land near spike 2's census. Any "failures" above zero, or
; GetSurfaceLevel-on-shadowed climbing, names the problem directly - the last
; one is the known gap: the engine locking a surface instead of the texture
; would bypass the shadow entirely.
;
;   0 = plain D3D9, MANAGED untouched - the known-good path       <- default
;   1 = D3D9Ex + pool translation
;
; If the game fails to start, set this back to 0. No rebuild needed.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
D3D9ExMode=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ZeroCopy   -- stage 2: what the D3D9Ex upgrade was FOR (run 39)
;
; Only takes effect when D3D9ExMode=1 - a shared surface cannot be created on a
; plain D3D9 device - so leaving this at 1 changes nothing on its own.
;
;   before   GetRenderTargetData (GPU->sysmem, blocks) + LockRect (DMA lands)
;            + memcpy 35 MB + Map + CopyResource        ~9.8 ms at 4096x2160
;   after    StretchRect backbuffer -> shared RT (GPU->GPU)
;            + CopyResource shared -> XR swapchain      target: under 1 ms
;
; The frame never touches the CPU. Spike 1 proved every link in a 32-bit
; process: a D3D9Ex texture with a shared handle, opened by D3D11's
; OpenSharedResource, pixels intact.
;
; Synchronisation is the only genuinely new problem. D3D9 has no counterpart to
; D3D11's keyed mutex, so nothing makes the D3D11 read wait for the D3D9 write.
; A D3DQUERYTYPE_EVENT fence issued AFTER the StretchRect and waited on before
; the D3D11 copy is what spike 1 used and what is used here. That wait is not
; new overhead - run 35 measured it at 2-4 ms of genuine rendering, and a frame
; cannot be shown before it is drawn. What disappears is the ~9.4 ms of
; transfer and memcpy piled on top.
;
; The A/B is D3D9ExMode=1 with ZeroCopy=0 against ZeroCopy=1.
;
; Note the cull-band diagnostic goes inert under zero-copy - there are no
; CPU-readable pixels any more. Set ZeroCopy=0 if you need to measure it.
;
;   1 = shared surface, GPU->GPU                                  <- default
;   0 = CPU round-trip through system memory
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ZeroCopy=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; SplitColourTargetsOnly   -- the one-eye shadow test (run 40)
;
; Shadows appear in one eye only and are wrong in that eye. The render-target
; census has been flagging the likely cause for several runs:
;
;   2A330540  4096x2160  A16B16G16R16F   9337 draws  <- SPLIT
;   2A330240  4096x2160  A8R8G8B8         740 draws  <- SPLIT
;   2A330840  4096x2160  other            240 draws  <- SPLIT
;   ^ 3 SCENE-SIZED surfaces split. Only the two colour targets should be.
;
; The scene test is SIZE EQUALITY alone, so a third scene-sized surface -
; neither colour target - gets split 240 times a frame.
;
; Why that would break shadows specifically: a screen-space effect that
; reconstructs world position from DEPTH uses an inverse-projection constant we
; do not remap. Feed it depth from geometry we DID remap and the reconstruction
; lands somewhere else - differently per eye, since each eye's remap differs.
; Which is exactly the reported symptom.
;
; Setting this to 1 splits only the two known scene colour formats, excluding
; that third surface. If the shadows change, it is implicated. If they do not,
; the cause is elsewhere and this cost one relaunch.
;
;   0 = split every scene-sized target, as before                 <- default
;   1 = split only the two scene colour targets
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SplitColourTargetsOnly=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ExtraCamReg   -- TRIED AND FAILED (run 43). Kept so the result is reproducible.
;
; Everything in the matrix path keys off ONE locked register, so exactly one
; view-projection is ever remapped per eye. Run 42's constant dump found a
; second one at vs c13 - identical projection columns to c0-c3, but a
; world-space translation where c3 is translated-world and small.
;
; Setting this to 13 remaps it too. Result: geometry stretched to the edge of
; the screen AND the shadows were unchanged.
;
; Both halves are informative. The stretching proves c13 really does drive
; on-screen vertices - it is not a dead register or a light's transform. But the
; shadows not moving at all argues c13 is NOT the carrier for the mis-projected
; shadow geometry, so the shadow's cause is elsewhere.
;
; The stretch itself is explainable: the remap assumes c13 shares c0's storage
; convention and skips ApplyProjection. Either could be wrong, and a world-space
; matrix may not accept clip-space eye remapping the way a translated-world one
; does. Sorting that out is its own project.
;
;   -1 = off                                                      <- default
;   13 = also remap vs c13 (reproduces the run-43 failure)
;
; If head tracking or geometry ever looks wrong, set this to -1 and relaunch.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ExtraCamReg=-1

HookUserPointerDraws=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HeadRoll   -- the third rotation axis, finally written
;
; F9 drove pitch and yaw only. Roll was never written anywhere, so tilting your
; head sideways left the horizon glued to the headset - the last missing piece
; of a real head pose.
;
; It does NOT go through the engine's rotation. The detour owns pitch, yaw needs
; a direct write to the controller, and UE3 zeroes camera roll in several
; places - three seams to fight for an angle we already own outright, since the
; view matrix is ours by the time it reaches the GPU. Roll is applied there, as
; a rotation of clip x/y, after the forced projection and before the per-eye
; half-frame remap.
;
; ---- why the compositor does not already do this ----
;
; The submitted projection layer carries the head's full orientation, roll
; included, so it looks like the runtime should be rotating the image for us. It
; does not: a projection layer is reprojected by the DELTA between the pose we
; claim and the pose the display is at. Claim a rolled pose for an unrolled
; render while the head is at that same roll, the delta is zero, and the image
; is presented straight - exactly the symptom. Baking roll into the render is
; what makes the claimed pose true.
;
; Costs nothing measurable: about 16 multiply-adds on the camera-matrix uploads
; only, and it allocates nothing. Confirmed in the headset at up to 43 degrees
; of tilt with no corner dropout.
;
; NUMPAD1 toggles it live. NUMPAD2 flips the sign. The default of -1 was
; MEASURED - +1 tilted the horizon the wrong way - and it is the third axis
; where OpenXR's handedness comes out negative against the game's, which is what
; F4 and F5 exist for on pitch and yaw.
;
; The maths is verified as arithmetic rather than by eye: spikes/roll_math
; asserts the rotation is exact, does not shear despite tanX != tanY, and
; composes with the eye remap. Run .\spikes\roll_math\build.ps1 - no game and no
; headset needed.
;
; Known interaction, not yet a problem: the engine culls with an UNROLLED
; frustum, so a rolled view's corners reach outside it. Nothing was observed at
; 43 degrees; PAGE UP is the lever if it ever shows up.
;
;   1 = tilting your head tilts the view                           <- default
;   0 = off, the horizon stays locked to the headset
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HeadRoll=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; OcclusionQueryMode   -- THIS IS THE FIX FOR THE VANISHING OBJECTS (run 30)
;
; UE3 draws a bounding box per primitive inside an occlusion query, reads the
; pixel count back, and skips drawing that primitive later if it came back
; zero. In true stereo that readback is wrong, and it was wrong on a huge
; scale: with the results forced to "visible", draws per frame went from ~1000
; to 2100-3800 and every reported object came back solid. The engine was
; throwing away 50-70% of its own draw calls.
;
; "Visible" is conservative: it can never wrongly delete geometry, it can only
; fail to save work. It is however BRUTE FORCE - a proper fix exists and is
; written up in STATUS.md. Do not read this as the final design.
;
; The cost is small and measured, for now. Frame rate held at 37-40 fps with
; peaks of 70, unchanged, because this build is bottlenecked on the CPU frame
; copy rather than on draw calls. Once the D3D9Ex zero-copy path removes that
; bottleneck, ~7,600 real draw calls a frame will matter and this should be
; revisited.
;
;   0 = auto: report visible while true stereo is on, normal in mono  <- default
;   1 = always report visible
;   2 = never override; the engine's own occlusion culling stays live
;   3 = refuse to create them                   ** CRASHES THIS BUILD **
;
; Mode 3 returns D3DERR_NOTAVAILABLE, the documented answer for an unsupported
; query type and the path UE3 takes on hardware without the feature. Run 30
; tried it and the game died between the splash screens and the main menu -
; consistent with the unchecked-NULL-from-a-factory habit already recorded for
; Raven's code. Kept only so nobody retries it.
;
; ---- run 157: ON THE VR PANEL, and modes 0-2 only ----
;
; Tested across the opening area, mode 2 dropped more frames than mode 0 - a
; comparison that can only be made by playing the same stretch both ways, which
; is why it moved to the panel. OCCLUSION reads AUTO / DRAW ALL / ENGINE CULL
; for modes 0, 1 and 2. Mode 3 is unreachable from the panel because it crashes;
; it is settable here only so the fact stays recorded.
;
; ⚠️ The GetData vtable patch is now installed UNCONDITIONALLY. It used to be
; skipped when this started at 2, which meant switching to 0 or 1 later did
; nothing at all - the hook the new mode needs was never installed, and there is
; no second chance. On mode 2 the patch is a plain pass-through and costs
; nothing. If either patch fails, the mode is forced to 2 and the log says so,
; because with no hook the engine's own culling is what is actually running and
; the panel must not display a mode that cannot happen.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OcclusionQueryMode=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Debug   -- the developer scaffolding, on/off (run 157)
;
; Everything this mod grew while being built: the on-screen state readout, and
; forty-odd hotkeys for toggling and tuning. None of it belongs in front of
; someone playing the game.
;
;   1 = readout drawn, every keyboard key live                   <- default
;   0 = readout hidden, keyboard inert except BACKSPACE
;
; Only an advanced user needs any of those keys, and for everyone else the
; controller is the whole interface. The VR panel is unaffected: it is opened by
; HOLDING a controller button and driven by the thumbstick, so it never went
; through the keyboard gate, and DEBUGGING on the panel turns this back on from
; inside the headset without editing a file or relaunching.
;
; ⚠️ EXACTLY ONE KEY IS EXEMPT: **BACKSPACE**, VR mode on/off. Every other key is
; recoverable - turn DEBUGGING back on and they return - but switching in and out
; of VR is not scaffolding, and the cold start is flat by design (run 37 reverted
; default-on) with no ENABLE VR setting yet, so gating it would leave a Debug=0
; launch unable to enter VR at all.
;
; F8 is NOT exempt. The controller's MENU tap already recentres, so losing it
; costs nothing.
;
; Implemented as one gate, not a conditional wrapped round eight hundred lines:
; every key reads through DebugKey() instead of GetAsyncKeyState(). There is one
; exception inside Present and it is BACKSPACE, so any other raw GetAsyncKeyState
; there is a bug and greps as one.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Debug=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; FrameCopyMode   -- the performance measurement (run 31)
;
; Getting the game's frame from D3D9 into D3D11 is a CPU round-trip, and it has
; been called this project's bottleneck since spike 2 without ever being
; broken down. It is three costs, not one:
;
;   readback      GetRenderTargetData, GPU -> system memory
;   lock/memcpy   system memory -> the D3D11 upload texture, ~14 MB at 1440p
;   upload        CPU -> GPU again, into the OpenXR swapchain image
;
; Only the FIRST is what D3D9Ex + a D3DPOOL_MANAGED wrapper would remove, and
; that wrapper is a large job - spike 2 measured 10,454 MANAGED allocations in
; one session, every one needing a backing store we own. So it is worth knowing
; which of the three actually costs before writing it.
;
; The per-second "frame copy" log line now reports all three plus the residual
; (frame time minus the copy). Read it before touching anything else.
;
;   0 = pipelined: issue this frame's readback, consume the one from last
;       frame. Costs one frame of latency. MEASURED SLOWER - see below.
;   1 = immediate: capture and consume in the same Present.        <- default
;
; ---- run 32 measured this, and pipelining lost ----
;
;     draws   pipelined            immediate
;       367   107.0 fps (9.34ms)   109.7 fps (9.12ms)
;       444   111.0 fps (9.01ms)   111.3 fps (8.98ms)
;       138   114.5 fps (8.74ms)   117.5 fps (8.51ms)
;
; Immediate is equal or better every time AND has no added latency, so it is
; the default. Mode 0 is kept only so the result can be reproduced.
;
; The interesting part is WHY. Pipelining did not remove the readback stall,
; it relocated it - pipelined reports copy 0.89ms + "our work" 9.4ms, immediate
; reports copy 4.2ms + "our work" 3.7ms, same total. Issuing
; GetRenderTargetData without locking its result lets the call return at once,
; so the phase timer sees nothing, but the driver still owes the transfer and
; it surfaces later inside work that is not broken out. Moving a stall out of
; an instrument's view is not an optimisation.
;
; The latency in mode 0 is handled honestly: each captured frame carries the
; eye, head pose and projection it was actually drawn with, and those are what
; get submitted. Do not "simplify" that by reading the live pose at submit
; time - handing the compositor a fresh pose for a stale image makes
; reprojection correct towards a position the frame never rendered from, which
; reads as swim rather than as lag.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FrameCopyMode=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; GpuFenceProbe   -- does the MANAGED wrapper actually buy anything? (run 34)
;
; The "copy" figure is NOT all round-trip. GetRenderTargetData does not just
; transfer, it SYNCHRONISES - it cannot return until the GPU has finished
; rendering the frame. So the number is really:
;
;     (wait for the GPU to finish)  +  (the actual GPU->CPU transfer)
;
; Only the second is what a zero-copy D3D9Ex path removes. The first is real
; rendering work that would still have to be waited for, just inside the
; compositor instead of inside us.
;
; This matters because the entire case for the D3DPOOL_MANAGED wrapper - weeks
; of work, 10,454 allocations, failure mode "the game will not start" - rests
; on which of the two the ~12.5 ms at 4096x2160 actually is. They cannot be
; told apart from a single merged number.
;
; With this on, an EVENT fence is issued and waited on BEFORE the readback, so
; the sync is paid somewhere it can be timed. The log then reports:
;
;   round-trip split: gpu wait 8.10 ms (rendering - NOT recoverable)
;     | transfer 2.90 + memcpy 2.30 + upload 0.00 = 5.20 ms RECOVERABLE
;     -> zero-copy ceiling 10.80 ms/frame (93 fps)
;
; RECOVERABLE is a CEILING on what the wrapper can buy, not a promise - the
; compositor still has to consume the shared surface.
;
; This adds no work; GetRenderTargetData was going to wait for the GPU anyway.
; It only moves the wait where it can be seen. If total frame time changes when
; you toggle this, the probe is lying - and that is exactly the failure that
; caught out FrameCopyMode, where a stall left the instrument without ever
; leaving the frame.
;
;   1 = split gpu wait from transfer                               <- default
;   0 = off, copy stays one merged number
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GpuFenceProbe=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TimeOurWork -- what does OUR code actually cost? (runs 206-211)
;
; Times StereoPair and the mesh matchers and reports both beside the frame
; budget. This is the tool that found the runs 206-210 framerate tanks, and it
; is kept for the next frame-budget question.
;
; ⚠️ OFF by default, and deliberately: it is the only diagnostic here whose cost
; scales with DRAW COUNT rather than with frames -- two QueryPerformanceCounter
; calls per StereoPair and two per mesh-match, at the 3,000-4,400 draws/frame
; this game reaches. A closed investigation should not charge rent on every run.
;
; ⚠️ The overhead has NOT been measured, so do not quote a figure. Measuring it
; means an A/B of 0 against 1 at a fixed spot -- the instrument cannot measure
; its own cost, which is the same class of error that let SSW silently halve
; every frame-rate number for thirty runs.
;
; ⚠️ Read "our work" in the budget as a RESIDUAL, not a measurement. It absorbs
; anyone's latency, including the game's whole frame, while looking exactly like
; our own bug.
;
;   0 = off                                                       <- default
;   1 = time StereoPair and the mesh matchers
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TimeOurWork=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 🚨 BEFORE TRUSTING ANY PERFORMANCE NUMBER: TURN OFF SSW
;
; Virtual Desktop's Synchronous Spacewarp (and Meta's ASW) pins the application
; at HALF the headset refresh rate on purpose and synthesises the frames in
; between. Nothing in the log looks wrong when it is on - the timings are
; honest, the frame itself is synthetic.
;
; It cost this project thirty runs. Every frame-rate figure recorded before run
; 32 - "~37 fps at 1440p", the bimodal distribution, the CPU-bound-stall
; diagnosis, and run 31's decision to cancel the D3D9Ex work - was measured
; through that halving. With SSW off at 120 Hz the same build runs at 105-118
; fps and there was no performance problem at all.
;
; The build now logs the display period on the first frame and warns when the
; frame time sits at ~2x it. Check for that line before reading anything else:
;
;   headset display period 8.33 ms (120.0 Hz)
;   *** frame time is 2.00x the display period. SUSPECT SSW/ASW ... ***
;
; Record the refresh rate and the SSW setting next to any number worth keeping.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; There is deliberately no resolution setting here.
;
; Forcing the D3D9 backbuffer does NOT raise UE3's render resolution - the
; engine sizes its scene targets from its own configured resolution, so
; overriding the backbuffer only desynchronises the two and switches stereo off.
;
; Pass it on the command line instead, which the engine reads into its own
; system settings:
;
;   Singularity.exe -ResX=3840 -ResY=2160 -windowed
;
; -windowed is required because fullscreen clamps to real display modes.
; 4992x2688 is refused; the cap appears to be near 4096.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] - aim mode 7's gun pose (the two numbers that need calibrating)
;
; Aim mode 7 (NUMPAD . once from off) is route 2 in full: the bullet follows
; your controller via GetAdjustedAim's return value, and the GUN follows it via
; SetAim's two float parameters, which feed the in-view arms AnimTree. The
; engine's own rotation is never written, so culling, LOD and draw distance
; stay on your head - pointing your hand at the floor cannot black out the
; ceiling the way aim mode 1 does.
;
; SetAim takes a normalised aim offset, conventionally +-1 over a range that is
; authored per-profile inside the AnimTree. That range is not readable from
; here, so these three are a GUESS with a calibration path rather than a
; setting anyone got right in advance.
;
; The log prints the game's own X and Y next to the view pitch that produced
; them, on every mode 7 run:
;
;   aim pose #7: game (0.000, 0.412) view pitch 5210 -> wrote (0.153, 0.412) ...
;
; The game's own Y divided by its own view pitch IS the units-per-1.0 wanted,
; so one run's log sets AimPoseUUPer1 exactly. Symptoms:
;
;   gun swings too far / not far enough  -> AimPoseUUPer1 (bigger = less swing)
;   gun swings the WRONG WAY             -> AimPoseSignX or AimPoseSignY = -1
;
; [Input]
; AimPoseUUPer1=16384      ; engine rotation units per 1.0 of aim offset (16384 = 90 deg)
; AimPoseSignX=1           ; -1 to mirror the horizontal swing
; AimPoseSignY=1           ; -1 to mirror the vertical swing
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] AimFieldSet - which fields aim mode 9 may write during a shot
;
; Run 99 wrote all seven scanned rotation fields and the bullet followed the
; controller in PITCH for the first time - but the camera moved on every shot.
; Both are the same story: yaw is very likely decoupling too and cannot be
; seen, because the camera jumps to the hand's yaw at the instant of the shot,
; so the bullet lands on the crosshair because the CROSSHAIR MOVED. Pitch
; survives because the mod's external-delta fold-in is yaw-only.
;
; The camera movement is not a race - it survived the seqlock. It is a write
; the engine ACTS on: the camera has mDesiredRotationInterpRate, so a
; desired-rotation field written even briefly makes the camera interpolate
; toward the hand over the following frames.
;
;   AimFieldSet=0   controller only: +0x0060 and +0x05D4, no camera writes
;   AimFieldSet=1   the above plus mCurrentPOV.Rotation (camera +0x042C)
;   AimFieldSet=2   every scanned field - this is run 99's behaviour, and the
;                   one that moves the camera
;
; Start at 0. If the bullet stops tracking pitch there, the aim is carried by
; a camera field and 1 then 2 narrow which one.
;
; [Input]
; AimFieldSet=0
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] - the gun visual (aim mode 11/12 + APPS)
;
; These are DEFAULTS IN CODE, so a missing key is not a bug - it just means the
; built-in value is used. They are listed here because tuning them is easier
; when they exist in the file to edit.
;
; Signs are 1 or -1. Do NOT hunt them by editing and restarting: CAPS LOCK
; cycles all eight rotation-sign combinations at runtime and logs each one, so
; the right combination is found inside a single session and written here
; afterwards to make it stick.
;
; [Input]
; GunSignYaw=1
; GunSignPitch=1     ; combo 2 in run 125 - found with CAPS LOCK, not by guessing
; GunSignRoll=1
; GunSignPosZ=-1
; GunFollowPosition=1     ; 0 freezes position, so rotation can be tuned alone
;
; The anchor is where the gun sits relative to your eye, in engine units
; (1 unit is about 1.9 cm), measured forward / right / up. It is the point the
; gun ROTATES ABOUT. Getting it near the grip is what stops the gun swinging
; through an arc when you turn your wrist - rotating about the eye instead of
; the handle was run 124's bug.
;
;   gun orbits a point IN FRONT of it  -> increase GunAnchorFwd
;   gun orbits a point BEHIND it       -> decrease GunAnchorFwd
;
; GunAnchorFwd=30      ; tuned by eye in run 131 with the on-screen marker
; GunAnchorRight=9
; GunAnchorUp=-13
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] - turning with the right thumbstick
;
; TurnMode picks HOW the stick turns you. All three are motion-controller
; turning; they differ in who owns the rate.
;
;   0  hand the stick to the engine's own look axis. Smooth, but the speed is
;      Raven's sensitivity curve, which this mod cannot see or set - so
;      TurnSpeed does nothing in this mode.
;   1  SNAP (default). Flick the stick, jump TurnAngle degrees. The most
;      comfortable option for most people, and the one to keep if you are at
;      all prone to motion sickness.
;   2  SMOOTH, done by the mod at exactly TurnSpeed degrees per second. Frame
;      rate independent, and it keeps working during cutscenes - modes 0 and 1
;      both go through paths the engine ignores while a scripted camera runs.
;
; Stick deflection is squared in mode 2, so a small push turns slowly and the
; top of the stick gives the full rate. TurnSpeed is clamped to 10-360.
;
; TurnSign=-1 if right turns you left. It applies to all three modes.
;
; [Input]
; TurnMode=1
; TurnAngle=30       ; degrees per flick, mode 1 only
; TurnSpeed=120      ; degrees per second, mode 2 only
; TurnSign=1
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HudStereo  -- the game's HUD and menus in BOTH eyes (run 151)
;
; Without it, health, ammo, the crosshair, pickup prompts and the menus are
; drawn ONCE at full-frame coordinates, so half lands in each eye and none of
; it is readable in the headset.
;
; The UI is Scaleform, submitted through DrawPrimitiveUP with its stage->clip
; matrix in vs c5-c8. This applies the same per-eye clip-space squash the scene
; geometry gets to those four registers and issues the draw once per eye.
;
; Two things are deliberately EXCLUDED, and both were learned by breaking them:
;   * fullscreen quads - remapping the scene's composite quad puts the whole
;     side-by-side frame into each half, i.e. four panels.
;   * offscreen render targets - the main menu is diegetic, rendered into a
;     texture and mapped onto a monitor in the scene. Squashing that put two
;     menus inside the texture.
;
; Costs 12-31 extra draw calls a frame against 700-1800, i.e. nothing.
;
; ⚠️ Requires HookUserPointerDraws=3 - that is how the UI reaches us at all -
; and raises it automatically rather than silently doing nothing.
;
;   1 = HUD and menus in both eyes                                <- default
;   0 = off, the UI spans the seam
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HudStereo=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] TriggerTouchGate -- the phantom trigger fix (run 156). THIS is the
;                             one that matters; AutoPad below is its fossil.
;
; ---- what was happening ----
;
; A Touch controller that has been set down and left to rest reports a
; SUSTAINED PARTIAL TRIGGER PULL - measured at left 0.34-0.43 and right
; 0.23-0.28, held for seconds, with isActive true on all 120 frames of every
; sample. It sails past the 0.10 deadzone and reaches the game as LT ~97 /
; RT ~64, and Raven's 360 layout maps those to AIM and FIRE. So the gun fires
; and aims by itself, and the engine does exactly what it was told.
;
; The frame-rate cost, from a session where the two states separate cleanly:
;
;   neither trigger        120 fps
;   right only (fire)       98 fps
;   both (fire + aim)       77 fps
;
; Over a whole session the correlation is total: every second with a nonzero
; trigger is 68-98 fps, every second without one is 109-120.
;
; ⚠️ A BIGGER DEADZONE IS NOT THE FIX, and it was considered and dropped. Real
; pulls read a hard 1.00 - nobody lingers at partial deflection - so a
; threshold near 0.6 would work today. But the phantom is not noise: it is a
; plausible number with no finger behind it, and it has been seen as high as
; 0.83. Any threshold that caught that would eat real input.
;
; ---- what the gate does ----
;
; Pulling a trigger requires touching it. `trigger/touch` is a capacitive
; sensor on the trigger itself, so if no finger is on it, the analog value did
; not come from the player whatever it claims. Bound to exactly two sources,
; per hand, and nothing else may ever be added - the moment it accepts a second
; surface it stops meaning "a finger is on the trigger" and becomes another
; copy of the broad `grasp` signal, which answers a different question.
;
; Where the binding does not resolve, values pass through unchanged: a runtime
; without the sensor keeps the old behaviour rather than losing its triggers.
; The log prints the veto count every second, so a run where the judder is gone
; and the count reads 0 means something ELSE fixed it.
;
;   TriggerTouchGate=1     on                                   <- default
;   TriggerTouchGate=0     off, for re-measuring the phantom
;
; ---- AutoPad: obsolete since run 156, and OFF by default ----
;
; Runs 67-75 chased this same judder for nine runs and never named it. Five
; mechanisms were proposed and all five died: OpenXR input cost, sleeping-
; controller runtime stalls, dwPacketNumber churn, XInput poll rate, and
; bSmoothFrameRate. The fallback was AutoPad - unplug the virtual pad once
; neither controller had been touched for AutoPadStillFrames frames, and plug
; it back the instant one was.
;
; It worked, for the reason now obvious: unplugging the pad is one way to stop
; the phantom trigger reaching the game. But it cost 1-2 SECONDS OF DEAD
; CONTROLS on every genuine pick-up, because UE3 enumerates XInput ports
; round-robin at about one per second. That latency was the whole reason this
; was reopened, and it is now simply gone.
;
; Kept in the build, not deleted: on a runtime where `trigger/touch` does not
; resolve, the veto cannot help and auto-pad is still the best answer there is.
;
;   AutoPad=0              off - the cause is fixed upstream    <- default
;   AutoPad=1              restore the old unplug/replug workaround
;   AutoPadStillFrames=600 ~5 s at 120 fps, only read when AutoPad=1
;
; ---- ThreadCpu: per-thread CPU census, bucketed by frame time ----
;
; Prints per-thread CPU time once a second, split into FAST (>=105 fps) and
; SLOW (<=90 fps) intervals, with the thread's start address as module+offset
; so it can be looked up in Ghidra. It answers "when the frame is slow, is the
; time BURNED or WAITED FOR" - a blocked thread accrues no CPU time.
;
; ⚠️ It was bucketed on the CONTROLLER STATE first, and that version reported
; "no difference" on a run where the user was watching the drop happen. The
; label was derived from the capacitive `grasp` sensor, which read `on desk`
; through ninety seconds of deliberately holding both controllers - one sample
; has a hand moving at 1331 um/frame and still reporting `on desk`. Both
; buckets held both states and the difference averaged away. Keying on the
; frame time itself removes every sensor from the loop.
;
;   ThreadCpu=1            on, costs a few microseconds a second <- default
;   ThreadCpu=0            off
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input]
; TriggerTouchGate=1
; AutoPad=0
; ThreadCpu=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] AimMethod   -- the one control the other four follow (run 158)
;
;   0 = HEAD               aim mode 0, arms visible, gun in front of your face,
;                          crosshair meaningful                     <- default
;   1 = MOTION CONTROLLER  aim mode 11, arms hidden, gun on the controller,
;                          crosshair meaningless
;
; On the VR panel as AIM METHOD, first row, because MOVE DIRECTION and CROSSHAIR
; both follow from it and it reads top-down as cause before effect.
;
; Every piece of this already worked. What was missing was one place to say which
; arrangement you want, and any way at all to say it without a keyboard - the aim
; mode was never read from the ini, so every session started at 0 and had to be
; walked back to 11 by hand.
;
; ---- what it deliberately does NOT touch ----
;
; It is not an input-device switch. Both methods take keyboard, mouse, a real 360
; pad, or the Touch controllers acting as one, and Raven's own input code handles
; all of them. Three behaviours that look like they belong here fall out for free
; and are left alone:
;
;   * Mouse pitch does nothing in either method, and no code makes that happen.
;     g_wantPitch is recomputed from the headset every frame and written over
;     whatever the mouse did. Yaw survives only because the yaw fold-in adds
;     external deltas into g_baseYaw, and there is no pitch fold-in.
;   * TurnMode already only affects controller play. g_padTurnAccum is fed from
;     the XR turn action's X axis alone; the mouse reaches the view through the
;     fold-in and never touches it.
;   * Snap turn works in HEAD too, because turning runs into g_baseYaw without
;     consulting the aim mode. Touch controllers as an untracked gamepad with
;     snap turn is a supported combination, not an accident.
;
; The right stick's vertical axis is not a look axis in either method: up is
; StickUp (CycleWeapon) and down is StickDown (Health), both configurable.
;
; ---- WalkDirection is PRESET, not forced ----
;
; Choosing HEAD sets MOVE DIRECTION to HEAD, because off-hand-relative movement
; is meaningless when you are not aiming with a hand. It is applied when you
; CHANGE the method and NOT when the ini is read - otherwise a saved
; WalkDirection would be overwritten on every launch and "preset" would be a lie.
;
; ---- cutscenes ----
;
; Both methods want ROT ENGINE with the yaw fold-in on, which is cutscene mode 1,
; the shipped arrangement. The cutscene auto-switch saves whatever mode it finds,
; forces mode 3 so head yaw survives the scripted camera, and restores on exit -
; so it is agnostic to the aim method and neither one changes it.
;
; ⚠️ Changing AIM METHOD *during* a cutscene writes the SAVED mode rather than the
; live one. Writing the live mode would be overwritten seconds later by the
; restore, and the setting would look like it had not stuck.
;
; ---- ⚠️ the mesh latch, which is what makes this work at all ----
;
; The gun and arms are identified by VERTEX COUNT, latched from the foreground
; pass. That latch used to run only when the APPS cycle passed through mode 1, so
; selecting a mode directly left the counts at zero, FgHidden and FgMoved both
; returned false, and the arms stayed visible while the panel reported MOTION
; CONTROLLER. Silent no-op.
;
; It is now its own function, called on any nonzero mode and RETRIED every frame
; until it takes - necessary because AimMethod=1 is applied from the ini long
; before any foreground pass has been drawn. The log prints "mesh latch: gun=N
; verts, arms=N verts" on the frame it succeeds. If that line never appears while
; MOTION CONTROLLER is selected, the gun and arms are not being touched at all.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input]
; AimMethod=0
;
; ---- BACKSPACE hands the game back completely ----
;
; AimMethod is the SETTING; whether it APPLIES is gated on VR mode. With VR mode
; off, aim mode returns to 0 and the arms and gun to normal, so the plain game is
; genuinely plain - mouse, keyboard and pad all behave as they always did, and the
; crosshair comes back even on AUTO. BACKSPACE again restores whichever method you
; chose. The setting is never cleared, so there is nothing to re-select.
;
; This was broken until run 158b: SetVrMode wrote head tracking, 6-DOF, stereo and
; projection and left aim mode 11 and the hidden arms in place. Flat mode ran with
; missing arms, a gun transformed onto a controller possibly sitting on a desk, and
; shots that did not go through the crosshair - and nothing said so. The restore
; now falls out of the same function that applies it, called from SetVrMode in both
; directions, so there is no separate teardown that can drift out of step.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; StartInVr   -- VR mode on at launch, without pressing BACKSPACE (run 159)
;
;   1 = VR mode on as soon as the headset is up                   <- default
;   0 = cold start is flat, press BACKSPACE (run 37's behaviour)
;
; Also on the VR panel as VR MODE, which is the same switch BACKSPACE throws.
; BACKSPACE still works and is one of the two keys exempt from Debug.
;
; ---- ⚠️ this is run 36 again, and run 37 reverted run 36 ----
;
; Run 36 made VR mode default-on and run 37 reverted it, for a good reason worth
; repeating: default-on meant THE MAIN MENU WAS RENDERED FRAME-SPLIT for the
; first time ever, completely untested, on every launch.
;
; What changed is specific rather than a change of mind. Run 151 put the game's
; HUD and menus into both eyes - the pause menu and prompts are explicitly on
; that list - so the thing run 37 objected to is the thing run 151 built. The
; premise went stale rather than the decision being wrong, which is the first
; lesson at the top of STATUS.
;
; But "should work now" is not "was tested now". If the menus look wrong at
; launch, StartInVr=0 restores run 37's cold start exactly, with no rebuild.
;
; ---- no headset, no split ----
;
; Applied at the first Present and gated on an OpenXR session EXISTING, not at
; device creation. With no headset connected there is no session, and turning VR
; mode on anyway would split the frame side-by-side on the desktop with nothing
; to send it to - the run-36 failure in a different costume, on a machine where
; nothing can fix it. A missing headset leaves the game flat and logs:
;
;   StartInVr=1 but there is no OpenXR session after 600 frames - staying flat.
;
; ---- what saves it, and what does not ----
;
; The panel's VR MODE row saves on close, so the state you leave it in is the
; next cold start. BACKSPACE does NOT save: it is a toggle, not a setting, and a
; key you press to peek at the flat game should not silently change how the game
; launches tomorrow.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
StartInVr=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; VideoStereo   -- Bink video in both eyes, correctly (run 160)
;
;   1 = video drawn once per eye                                 <- default
;   0 = off: video spans the seam, i.e. double vision + over-zoom
;
; ---- the symptom, which was two reports of one bug ----
;
; The splash screens showed DOUBLE VISION and the opening cutscene was "way too
; zoomed in". Same artifact. A fullscreen quad is passed through untouched, which
; is right for UE3's post-process quads because their source ALREADY holds the
; side-by-side frame. A Bink frame is a SINGLE image, so passed through it covers
; both halves with one copy: left eye gets the left half, right eye the right
; half. Different content per eye, each stretched over a whole eye.
;
; ---- the classifier, measured rather than guessed ----
;
; Every video frame in the probe read identically:
;
;   DrawPrimUP type 5 prims 2 stride 24 v0 (-0.957,+1.000)
;              tex 1280x720 fmt 50  rt 4992x2688     ... 1 draw(s) this frame
;
; fmt 50 is D3DFMT_L8 - Bink decodes to Y/U/V planes as luminance textures and
; combines them in a pixel shader.
;
; THREE conditions, all required. "Texture smaller than the render target" alone
; is WRONG: it also matches every bloom and depth-of-field downsample pass, which
; are fullscreen quads over half-res buffers. What excludes those is FIRST DRAW
; OF THE FRAME - a post-process pass runs after hundreds of scene draws, and a
; video frame contains exactly one draw.
;
;   1. first draw of the frame
;   2. 2-primitive quad in NDC (the existing UserPtrQuad classifier, reused)
;   3. stage-0 texture dimensions != render target dimensions
;
; ---- why BOTH axes are halved ----
;
; x' = x/2 + eyeOffset puts the quad in one half. y' = y/2 is NOT optional: each
; half is 2496x2688, nearly portrait, so halving x alone would squash 16:9 to
; 8:9. With both halved the pixels go 4777x2688 -> 2389x1344, still exactly 16:9,
; centred with black above and below. A flat screen in front of you, which is how
; 2D video should read in a headset.
;
; If the vertex layout is not one this can rewrite, it falls through and draws the
; quad exactly as before - an uncorrected video beats no video.
;
; ---- how to tell if it misfires ----
;
; The detection line is logged ONCE, which proves it fired and says nothing about
; where. The per-second count is the real instrument:
;
;   video quads drawn per eye: 120 this second
;
; In a cutscene that is expected. IN GAMEPLAY IT IS A MISCLASSIFICATION - a post
; process quad taken for video - and the line says so in as many words. That is
; the one way this can break gameplay, and VideoStereo=0 disables it without a
; rebuild.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VideoStereo=1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] CutsceneStickTurn -- stick turning during cutscenes (runs 161, 165)
;
; Renamed from CutsceneLockTurn, which was a double negative - "LockTurn=0" reads
; as both "no lock" and "turning locked off", and both readings came up in one
; conversation. This says what it does. On the VR panel under COMFORT.
;
;   0 OFF    no stick turning. Reorient by turning your body; physical rotation
;            does not cause the vection artificial rotation does, which matters
;            most in exactly these scenes where the camera is already moving you.
;   1 GAME   the stick is handed to the ENGINE's look axis for the duration, so
;            it goes down the same path as the mouse and inherits the scene's own
;            clamp. Overrides TurnMode for the cutscene only, so you keep snap
;            turn in gameplay and still get vanilla-consistent turning in scenes -
;            a combination that was not reachable before.
;   2 FREE   your normal TurnMode applies. Snap and smooth are mod-side and
;            therefore UNCLAMPED, which lets a standing player turn past what the
;            scene intended.                                       <- default
;
; HEAD LOOK IS UNAFFECTED at every setting - it comes from the mod's forced
; matrix and never touches the turn code.
;
; ⚠️ Applies to the STICK only, in both aim methods. The mouse is the game's own
; input and never passes through here. Naming it a "controller aim" setting would
; have made it silently inert while holding Touch controllers in head aim mode,
; which is a perfectly reachable combination.
;
; ⚠️ THE CLAMP DEPENDS ON TURN MODE, not on aim method. Mouse and TurnMode 0 both
; go through the engine's look axis and inherit the matinee's limits; TurnMode
; 1/2 are mod-side and do not. A 360 pad in head aim behaves like the mouse on
; GAME TURN and does not on snap or smooth.
;
; OFF is suppressed at the ACCUMULATOR and cleared, not at the point it is
; applied: blocking only the application would let deflection pile up for the
; length of the cutscene and dump it as one jump on exit. And not as an early
; return - buttons must still reach the game, one of them being the skip.
;
; ⚠️ None of this is what fixed starting a cutscene rotated 90 degrees. That was
; a base heading captured at the main menu, fixed in run 163 by anchoring it to
; the scripted camera.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] CutsceneLockCamera -- stop the scene rotating you (run 164)
;
;   0 = the base tracks the scripted camera, so the scene's pan carries you and
;       keeps its subject framed                                  <- default
;   1 = the base is frozen at the heading the cutscene STARTED on, so the scene
;       rotates around you instead
;
; The tradeoff, plainly: if you are sitting in a helicopter and you lock the
; camera, the helicopter moves around you. That is a fair trade for anyone who
; does not want to be swung about by an intense scene, and a bad default for
; everyone else - which is why it is off.
;
; The anchor latches on the first frame of the scene, NOT on the transition edge:
; the run-162 log caught the camera swinging -77 then +98 degrees within two
; frames of the edge, so latching there would freeze you to a transient.
;
; Applies to cutscenes only. Menus follow the camera unconditionally - freezing
; there would reintroduce the rotation run 168 fixed.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CutsceneStickTurn=2
CutsceneLockCamera=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Input] LeftHanded / SwapSticks -- handedness, as TWO settings (run 170)
;
; Split deliberately. Half-Life: Alyx shipped them welded together, players said
; it forced them to aim or move with the wrong hand, and Valve separated them in
; Update 1.2. This copies the fixed version, not the shipped one.
;
;   LeftHanded=0    gun, aim, triggers, grips and face buttons mirror to the
;                   other hand. STICKS DO NOT.                    <- default
;   SwapSticks=0    movement and turning trade sticks. Nothing else changes.
;
; LeftHanded needs a RELAUNCH and the panel row says so. OpenXR binding
; suggestions are frozen when the action set is attached, so a live toggle would
; move the gun and leave the buttons where they were. SwapSticks is live - it is
; four floats swapped at read time.
;
; ⚠️ MENU CANNOT MIRROR. `menu/click` exists on the LEFT hand only in the Touch
; profile and `system/click` on the right is reserved by the runtime - confirmed
; against the interaction profile. So the pause tap and the recentre long-press
; stay on the left thumb in both modes, and the startup log says so.
;
; The face buttons mirror as PAIRS - x<->a and y<->b - so jump/crouch and
; age/flashlight trade places with no collision. A mirror that swaps only the
; HAND produces /user/hand/left/input/a/click, which does not exist, and
; xrSuggestInteractionProfileBindings is all-or-nothing: one bad path silently
; kills every binding. That is why left-handed mode logs each mirrored path.
;
; ⚠️ GunAnchorRight is NOT mirrored. It was tuned with the gun attached to the
; ARMS, so it describes where the game's own gun mesh sits relative to your eye -
; and the game has no left-handed arms. Two attempts to mirror it were removed.
; If the gun swings through an arc with position frozen (TAB), the anchor is a
; PIVOT in the wrong place, not an offset with the wrong sign.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LeftHanded=0
SwapSticks=0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Render] WeaponFxRideGun -- the muzzle flash and barrel smoke (run 181)
;
;   1 = every draw in the first-person pass rides the same transform as the gun
;       - flash, barrel smoke, ejected shells                     <- default
;   0 = only the gun mesh moves; the effects stay where the engine put them
;   2 = TEST. Drops the whole first-person pass. The gun goes BLACK, which is
;       correct (run 117: the pass is composited through a mask, so removing the
;       geometry leaves the mask nothing to show). Read the SMOKE, not the gun -
;       it vanishing means it is in this pass, it surviving means it is not.
;
; ---- 🔬 run 181 result: engaged, and the barrel smoke was not in it ----
;
; `weapon fx: N ... (new peak)` climbed 0 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4, so draws beyond the gun
; mesh are in the pass and did ride the transform. The barrel smoke stayed at
; screen centre anyway; the impact smoke correctly did not move. So the widening
; is right as far as it goes and the smoke is somewhere else. Mode 2 exists to
; settle that by construction rather than by inference.
;
; Reported as "the smoke that should come out of the barrel is stuck in the
; middle of the screen", and it is not a bug in the gun transform - it is the
; transform's reach. Route 2 deliberately never moves the engine's own weapon
; (that is why culling and LOD cost nothing), so a muzzle effect spawns at the
; engine's muzzle, which is the centre of your view, while the mesh it is
; supposed to be leaving sits over on your controller.
;
; The rule: the foreground pass IS the first-person weapon pass. The engine
; separated that geometry for us in run 114, so everything in it belongs on the
; controller. Matching a single vertex count was always narrower than the thing
; being described. The arms remain the one exception - they are hidden, not
; moved.
;
; ⚠️ Nothing rides until the gun is LATCHED. Moving the effects onto the
; controller while the gun is still at the engine's position would separate them
; from the very mesh they are attached to, which looks worse than the bug.
;
; This is also the A/B, and BOTH answers mean something:
;
;   smoke follows the gun at 1 and returns to centre at 0
;       -> the effects are in the foreground pass and this is the fix.
;   no difference either way
;       -> they are drawn somewhere else, no transform on this path can reach
;          them, and the next seam is SetFlashLocation - which the run-95 census
;          already measured at 88.9% enrichment under fire.
;
; The log line `weapon fx: N foreground draw(s) rode the gun transform in one
; frame (new peak)` separates those two: N staying at its baseline through a
; magazine means the mechanism found nothing, not that the transform is wrong.
;
; ⚠️ The premise this replaces named the wrong effect, and chasing it cost
; sixty-odd runs. STATUS said "barrel smoke IS correctly aligned, so find what it
; rides on that the flash does not". The aligned smoke was the IMPACT smoke off
; the wall:
;
;   impact smoke   spawned in WORLD space at the trace hit point. Route 2 rotates
;                  the trace, so the hit point is right and so is the puff - free.
;   flash + barrel
;   smoke          attached to the FIRST-PERSON WEAPON, which the engine still
;                  has at the camera and route 2 never moves.
;
; So the flash and the barrel smoke are one system and always were. The asymmetry
; is real but it is world-space vs view-space, which is the first question to ask
; about any effect that lands in the wrong place.
;
; ⚠️ Impact smoke must therefore NOT move when this is on. It is world geometry in
; the main scene, not in the foreground pass, so it should be untouched - and it
; moving onto the gun would mean this setting is reaching further than the pass.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WeaponFxRideGun=1
